  "text": "Question three. What makes a dbt project AI-ready? Everything that helps a new analyst helps an assistant, with less tolerance for gaps, because an assistant won't ask. Describe every public model and column, including the grain, one row per order, and how to use it: for revenue, the net revenue metric. Test what the descriptions claim: the key unique and not null, relationships for foreign keys, and accepted values, so the assistant knows the real statuses are completed, refunded and cancelled. Mark staging and intermediate models private or protected, and expose only the public marts to the assistant. That removes the wrong choice, rather than hoping it makes the right one. Put every number the business argues about in the semantic layer. And check source freshness, so an answer can say as of when, and the assistant can refuse to answer from stale data. One caution: documentation is now an input. Drop the words and test accounts from the end of this description, and an assistant that follows it to the letter reports eleven hundred ninety. So if an assistant drafts the descriptions from the sequel, review them like code.",
